          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} Models
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theoretical+physics"} theoretical physics
            are often given in the form of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equations"} differential equations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+PDE"} integrability
            is the property of a concrete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
            which enables one to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solution"} solve
            these equations in a closed form, or in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratures"} quadratures
            (expressions in terms of ordinary
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrals"} integrals
          %h2#classical_integrable_systems Classical integrable systems
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liouville+integrability"} Liouville integrability
            = succeed "-commuting" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann-Hilbert+problem"} Riemann–Hilbert method
            and Birkhoff decomposition (which also provide connections to the subject of special functions)…
          %p
            Much of the knowledge about the algebras of differential operators on curves has been obtained from research on Calogero–Moser systems and generalizations (and their connections to the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KP+hierarchy"} KP hierarchy
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hitchin+fibration"} Hitchin system
          %h2#quantum_integrability Quantum integrability
          %p
            Often classically integrable systems after quantization may be also exactly solved, or show some other form of integrability, but there are no general rules when this is possible. Quantum integrability usually means that we can find a complete system of commuting operators whose common eigenstates will form a basis of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            of the model. Many examples are found making manipulations with algebras of operators; as a consequence generalizations of existing models often involve combinatorial data with algebaric meaning; say for example generalized Calogero models related to the root systems. Quantum R-matrices and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+group"} quantum groups
            were invented with a view toward quantum integrable systems systematizing analogues of a classical quantum integrability trick called the Bethe Ansatz.
